  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Warmer weather is expected through mid-week, followed by a \ncold front that will bring an increasing chance of showers \nThursday through Friday. \n\nThe coastal low off the southern New England coast will exit into \nthe northern Atlantic tonight. An upper level ridge and surface high \npressure will build across the eastern Great Lakes region through \ntonight. The mid-level ridge axis will move east of the forecast \narea Wednesday afternoon as a shortwave trough and cold front \napproach from the northwest. \n\nLingering low-level moisture from the coastal low will maintain \npartly cloudy conditions through this evening. Patchy fog is \nanticipated across the higher terrain. Mid to high level clouds will \nspread across the region Wednesday. Temperatures will rise well \nabove normal with highs in the low to mid 70s. \n\nA warm front will lift across the region Thursday morning. \nIsentropic lift and modest moisture may result in a brief window of \nscattered showers across western NY Thursday morning, and across \nnorth-central NY Thursday afternoon. Temperatures at 850mb will rise \nto +16C behind the front. Cloud cover from the upstream trough and \ncold front may limit the full potential for surface heating, however \nit will still be warmer than normal with high temperatures in the \nmid 70s. A 30-40knot low level jet will reside across the region \nThursday, and southwest flow will result in breezy conditions \ndownwind of the Lakes. Wind gusts up to 30 mph are possible, with \nweaker winds elsewhere. \n\nA stronger trough will dig into the Upper Great Lakes region \nThursday night and aide in pushing the shortwave trough and cold \nfront through the eastern Great Lakes region. A ribbon of\nvorticity, deep moisture, and low-level convergence will \nsupport showers Friday. There still remains uncertainty in the \namount of rainfall late Thursday night through Friday, however \nthe flood threat is near-zero. \n\nCold air advection will occur immediately behind the cold front \nFriday through Friday night. Surface high pressure will move across \nthe region through Saturday and mostly dry weather is expected. \nTemperatures will return to near normal values.",
